<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crisis of the Third Century</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/235">235</a>–<a href="/years/284">284 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crisis of the Third Century was a period of turmoil in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> from 235 to 284 CE. It was marked by rapid turnover of emperors, civil wars, invasions on several frontiers, plague and severe economic and monetary problems. The empire temporarily split into separate states before being restored and reorganised under Aurelian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crisis-of-the-third-century.jpg" alt="Crisis of the Third Century" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: historicair 23:27, 8 August 2007 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Ancient_Rome_271_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…fiction gave way under Diocletian (r. 284–305 CE) to an openly autocratic model, the Dominate, in which the emperor was addressed as dominus (lord) and surrounded by elaborate court ceremonial, formalising the military monarchy that had in practice replaced Augustan forms during the <b>Crisis of the Third Century</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It falls to half that strength only in 235, when the murder of Severus Alexander opened the half century of disorder known as the <b>Crisis of the Third Century</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li></ul></section>
